<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Berke</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1266">1266</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Berke Khan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Berke was khan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> from 1257 to 1266, a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> through Jochi. He was the first Mongol ruler of the Horde to adopt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, and he fought his cousin Hülegü of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, allying with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> sultanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> against him. His war marked the breakup of the united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/berke.jpg" alt="Berke" width="320" height="153" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG coins · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Golden_Horde._Berke._AH_655-665_AD_1257-1267_Qrim_(Crimea)_mint._Struck_circa_AH_662-665_(AD_1263-1267).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
